A||||9||Jefrysuko||Does anyone buy this?||||1||
Z||000000||Jefrysuko||06-06-2001||12:37 AM||||This came off the Fiero list.  Let me know what you think.<P>------------------------------------------<P>I have an 86.5 GT automatic (wife can't dive a stick shift).  I bought it<BR>new in April 1986 and drive it to and from work every business day.<P>Here is what has been done to it.<BR>The engine was changed out when it had 122,786 miles on it. Nothing wrong<BR>with the old engine except it could use a valve job.  The car now has 169k+<BR>miles on it.<P>The new engine is a 3.1 1990 Fiero engine.  No, I'm not kidding.  It is 1990<BR>3.1 Pontiac Fiero engine.<BR>I purchased this engine from American Dismantlers, located in Detroit, MI.<BR>in 1991.  It was originally sold to me as a new 2.8 Fiero V-6, year unknown.<BR>The engine was still strapped to it's assembly line pallet (MPV 9000 was<BR>stenciled on the steel pallet). American Dismantlers told me they had<BR>purchased all of Pontiac's remaining surplus factory Fiero parts inventory<BR>in late 89. Seats, fenders, everything which made up the cars.  The guy<BR>there said all of the stuff was gone except for a wiring harness, which I<BR>bought, some odds and ends and three of these palletized motors.  The engine<BR>was new and complete with spark plugs, plug harness, fuel injector harness,<BR>pulley's, brackets, etc.  But, there were several different items which were<BR>not listed in the P-22 parts book and GM denies the existence of these<BR>parts. (Note: I'll list all of the part numbers taken from the parts or ones<BR>added.)<P>1. Fuel rail (17089016) did not have a cold start connection on it.  GM rep<BR>stated it was going to be done away with for the 1990 production run because<BR>it is and was a leading cause of engine fires on the V-6 models.  The<BR>threaded hole on the lower manifold for the cold start sensor was filled in<BR>with a threaded plug.  The hole where the cold start injector was to go was<BR>filled with a soft plug and the wiring harness didn't have any wiring for a<BR>cold start device.<BR>2.  The EGR system had been changed to the "Digital" electronic type which<BR>GM introduced in 1990 (some late production 1989s had this on their V-6s<BR>instead of the solenoid/vacuum operated type). There was a casting<BR>(10089848A) mounted on the rear of the firewall side head with a digital EGR<BR>device mounted on it.  The stainless flex tubing was connected to the intake<BR>in the standard position and the other end to the mount casting.  The gasses<BR>were extracted via a stainless flex tube mounted on the other side of the<BR>EGR mount casting and fastened to the exhaust manifold header on the<BR>firewall side.  All of the plastic tubing was gone except for three vacuum<BR>tubes (10078943B).  The vacuum bosses on the upper manifold (10078958A) were<BR>blanked off with rubber caps. The vacuum tube which goes under the throttle<BR>body is slightly bigger than the one on my 86.<BR>3. The throttle body (Holly) (10070199) has a GM part number which is<BR>different from the parts book and GM states it's not a valid number.  Also<BR>on the throttle body, there isn't any place for a throttle position switch.<BR>4. The oil pressure sender unit (1643532) looks like on I had on my 1990<BR>Grand Prix.  Totally different from the standard Fiero one.<BR>5. The engine block had a anti-knock sender (10065459) unit installed<BR>although it still had the standard Fiero distributor on it.<BR>6. The recall on the V-6 engines (88-C-24)was already on this engine and<BR>this recall dated 8/15/1990.<BR>7. The fuel injector wiring harness was set up for inline injection.<BR>8. On the water pump (10044972), the pipe where the heater hose connects was<BR>blanked off with a threaded plug.<BR>9. The oil pan (number was painted over and couldn't read it) was one a GM<BR>parts manager stated didn't come out until early 1990.<BR>10. The flex plate is 1410055.<BR>11. The fuel injectors are 5235136.<P>Inside the block was a 3.1 crank (10078981).  Checked the part number with<BR>the GM performance parts book.  The pistons (10070115C) are dished slightly<BR>and the numbers stamped on the top do not reference with anything GM says<BR>they built.  There were weight numbers stamped on each piston where the<BR>wrist pin goes through.  58.7, 58.85, etc.  On the face of each valve was<BR>the numbers 1 through six written in colored grease pencil and also on the<BR>tops of the pistons.<P>Here is what was done to the engine by Mike Myers Racing Engines here in New<BR>Orleans:<P>1. Balanced engine.<BR>2. Painted all inside rough parts of the block and heads with Red Glyptal.<BR>3. Installed Crane's Compucam #254122-1, grind number 2030.<BR>4. Heads were checked for trueness, polished intakes and ground them out to<BR>gasket size as well as the three intake sections.<BR>5. Crane Gold series roller rockers (25750-12)installed.  Associated Crane<BR>valve springs (99848-12), Crane lifters (992686-12), and Crane push rods<BR>(25621-12).  Bronze valve guides installed.  Each valve stem was ground to<BR>open the same amount.  Each valve spring was measured to have the same<BR>tension as all the other ones and was shimmed when not in tolerance.  Every<BR>valve opens the same distance and closes at the same rate.<BR>6. GM high volume oil pump (10051104) was blue-printed.  The oil pressure<BR>relief spring was changed to open at 70 psi (GM 10044435).<BR>7. A remote oil filter is located in front of the right rear wheel.  I use a<BR>PF-35L filter.  Also mounted in this area is an oil cooler which draws air<BR>from a Mecham vented fender.<BR>8. I had to change all of the EGR pluming back to the vacuum type because of<BR>no electronics to run the electronic one.  **This is why I'm interested in<BR>your chip so I can convert the engine back to it's original configuration<BR>and do away with the EGR all together.<BR>9.  FOCOA headers installed and Jet Hot Coated.  All of the intake manifolds<BR>are Jet Hot coated as well.<BR>10. MSD-6A and MSD Blaster Coil.<P>According to Crane's specs the torque curve now peakes at 4500 rpm vice 3200<BR>rpm and the valve train can go to 6800 rpm safely.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeffrey D Suckow<BR>      <A HREF="mailto:Jefrysuko@aol.com">Jefrysuko@aol.com</A>      <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Zippy_11_sig.jpg">              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT_4.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||06-06-2001||02:54 AM||||First off, The decision to end the Fiero came long before they would even think about making/designing a "1990" engine. I can understand making 1989 prototype cars and having "suppliers" making parts for the 89 model year, But not 1990. They never even had a chance to get the "power steering" option going and that was suppose to be mid 88 option!<BR>Next, No cold start injector? GM was using cold start injectors all the way up to 1994. They may still be using them now. (don't know for sure but I would suspect that they still use them)<BR>As for a TB without a TPS sensor? How would the ECM know where the throttle was? They still use TPS sensors today. If they had come up with a way to rid them in 1990 we wouldn't have them today.<BR>Starting in 1989 all GM 60* V-6's had DIS ignition systems. So why does it still have the "standard Fiero distributor"? <BR>Finally, If this engine was the "Hot ticket" he makes it out to be, Why didn't the Z-24 and Sunbird GT's get it? Or even the bigger Grand Prix's or the plastic vans? (trans sport, lumina APV, and stilletto) The 3.1L engines were used on a wide variety of GM cars. He may have a 3.1L "prototype" engine but it is not a "1990 Fiero" engine. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||TRiAD||06-06-2001||06:09 AM||||Agreed. Plus, "recalls" are NOT done on assembly line engines, updates are made to the spec for new ones when a recall is called. <BR>Sounds like someone selling a bridge or something... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B><I>Michael ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</I></B><BR>-----------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88FieroGTwhitefscustomcroppedmini.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||06-06-2001||08:37 AM||||It sounds like a 3.1 prototype for the Fiero to me.  Timing is about right too in terms of engineering/production lead times.  Remember the 2.8 was scheduled to to 'go out' after the '89 model year.  I'm sure the management team would have been planning for changeover even in the midst of the Fiero cancellation just in case they needed to stay in production (contingency planning).  As for the cold start injector it was eliminated on some of the later 2.8s ('89)and on most if not all 3.1s in the '90 model year.  I don't think it was a fire hazard but a cost savings.  We all know the General is not concerned about public safety w/ regard to fires (eng & gas tank).  They are however concerned about the bottom line.<P>  So IMO, the story could have some merit but I think if so this was an engineering sample that was misplaced.  GM would have destroyed anything that was pre-production.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||06-06-2001||08:48 AM||||sounds like a skunk works engineering prototype - esp since the heater tubes and such things were plugged. <P> Ill bet they were messing around with changing the ECM to use the regular injectors for cold start, eliminating the 7th injector.||||reg||1
Z||000005||DecadenceR||06-06-2001||09:13 AM||||Or, could it be that someone's been "schmokin some phat schit" again....?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Alex4mula||06-06-2001||10:52 AM||||So if he bought just one and there were three available, that means there are two more. Check them out  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Steve Normington||06-06-2001||12:29 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Oreif:<BR><B>As for a TB without a TPS sensor? How would the ECM know where the throttle was? They still use TPS sensors today</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Doesn't a Mass Air Flow sensor elimanate a TPS?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||06-06-2001||12:38 PM||||The water pump connection was changed over for '87.<BR>In '86 cars the heater hose connects to the pump, in '87 cars that hole in the pump is plugged and the heater hose tees in elsewhere.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Oreif||06-06-2001||12:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Steve Normington:<BR><B> Doesn't a Mass Air Flow sensor elimanate a TPS?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, The MAF sensor measures the flow of air. It is sort of simular to the MAP sensor. The only difference is the MAP sensor is hooked to the intake and measures pressure. The MAF sensor is located on the tube or duct between the air filter and throttle body. The TPS and the MAP/MAF sensor work together to set-up the fuel delivery. ||||reg||1
